VERSION:2.0
PRODID:-//4AM Wisdom//Three Week Trial//EN
CALSCALE:GREGORIAN
METHOD:PUBLISH
X-WR-CALNAME:4AM Wisdom
X-WR-TIMEZONE:America/New_York
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260901@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260901T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260901T011200
SUMMARY:Silence is not empty
DESCRIPTION:John Cage\nIdea: Silence is not the absence of experience. Attention reveals events we habitually exclude as noise or nothing.\nContext: Cage's work on silence and listening; especially 4′33″ and his writings on sound.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260902@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260902T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260902T011200
SUMMARY:Can a game stay open?
DESCRIPTION:James P. Carse\nIdea: A finite game is played to conclude in victory. An infinite game is played so that play can continue. Ask what your present activity is trying to preserve: victory or possibility.\nSource: Finite and Infinite Games.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260903@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260903T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260903T011200
SUMMARY:Wholeness has degrees
DESCRIPTION:Christopher Alexander\nIdea: Places and things are not simply whole or fragmented. Their structure can intensify or diminish the life of the larger field around them.\nContext: Alexander's later work on centers and degrees of life; The Nature of Order.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260904@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260904T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260904T011200
SUMMARY:Hope contradicts now
DESCRIPTION:Jürgen Moltmann\nIdea: Hope is not merely confidence that present trends will turn out well. It can contradict the apparent finality of the present by orienting life toward what is promised but not yet actual.\nContext: Theology of Hope.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260905@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260905T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260905T011200
SUMMARY:The margin may be center
DESCRIPTION:Flannery O’Connor\nIdea: The grotesque can disclose realities that ordinary proportion conceals. Distortion is sometimes a way of making an invisible moral structure visible.\nContext: O'Connor's fiction and essays on the grotesque and the Christian novelist.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260906@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260906T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260906T011200
SUMMARY:What resists your map?
DESCRIPTION:Marshall McLuhan\nIdea: We notice the content carried by a medium and overlook the environment the medium creates. Look for the part of experience your conceptual map makes difficult to perceive.\nContext: McLuhan's distinction between media content and the transformations produced by media themselves.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260907@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260907T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260907T011200
SUMMARY:Become singular
DESCRIPTION:Søren Kierkegaard\nIdea: A crowd can relieve the individual of responsibility. Becoming a self requires standing personally before the demand one recognizes as true rather than borrowing conviction from numbers.\nContext: Kierkegaard's writings on 'the single individual' and the crowd.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260908@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260908T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260908T011200
SUMMARY:The wound can be a door
DESCRIPTION:Carl Jung\nIdea: What the conscious personality rejects may return as symptom, projection, or disturbance. The troublesome material can become an entrance into a larger self if it is encountered rather than merely expelled.\nContext: Jung's work on shadow and individuation.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260909@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260909T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260909T011200
SUMMARY:Usefulness has a shadow
DESCRIPTION:George MacDonald\nIdea: A thing need not justify itself by immediate utility. Imagination, story, play, and beauty may enlarge the person who later decides what counts as useful.\nContext: MacDonald's essays on imagination and fairy tales.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260910@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260910T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260910T011200
SUMMARY:Less can make more possible
DESCRIPTION:Buckminster Fuller\nIdea: Good design does not merely add resources. It can reorganize relationships so that less matter and energy support greater capability.\nContext: Fuller's idea of doing more with less and ephemeralization.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260911@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260911T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260911T011200
SUMMARY:Knowing can unmake wonder
DESCRIPTION:Gregory of Nyssa\nIdea: In the divine life, genuine knowledge does not terminate desire. Encounter enlarges the capacity for further encounter; fulfillment can deepen rather than extinguish longing.\nContext: Gregory's account of endless progress, often called epektasis, especially in The Life of Moses.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260912@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260912T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260912T011200
SUMMARY:Remove one certainty
DESCRIPTION:Pseudo-Dionysius the Areopagite\nIdea: Negation can be intellectually productive. To say what ultimate reality is not can loosen concepts that have quietly become substitutes for what they attempt to describe.\nContext: The Mystical Theology and the apophatic tradition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260913@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260913T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260913T011200
SUMMARY:What owns your attention?
DESCRIPTION:Evagrius Ponticus\nIdea: A recurring thought is not merely something you think. Repetition can give it a kind of agency over attention and action. Observe the thought's pattern before arguing with its content.\nContext: Evagrius's analysis of logismoi, recurring tempting or obsessive thought-patterns.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260914@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260914T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260914T011200
SUMMARY:A cell can teach
DESCRIPTION:Desert Fathers, especially Abba Moses and the sayings tradition\nIdea: Remaining in one place can expose evasions that movement disguises. Constraint sometimes becomes an instrument of perception.\nContext: The Sayings of the Desert Fathers repeatedly associate staying in one's cell with learning and transformation.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260915@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260915T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260915T011200
SUMMARY:Love alters perception
DESCRIPTION:Simone Weil\nIdea: Attention is not neutral inspection. The quality of attention can make another person's reality perceptible without immediately converting that person into a project, category, or instrument.\nContext: Weil's writings on attention, affliction, and love of neighbor.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260916@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260916T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260916T011200
SUMMARY:The ordinary has depth
DESCRIPTION:Julian of Norwich\nIdea: A small, vulnerable thing can disclose an unexpectedly large metaphysical horizon. Scale and significance need not correspond.\nContext: Julian's vision of a small object 'the quantity of a hazelnut' held in her hand, used to contemplate creation and divine love.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260917@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260917T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260917T011200
SUMMARY:Unknow what you know
DESCRIPTION:Meister Eckhart\nIdea: Spiritual and intellectual possession can become an obstacle. Detachment includes relinquishing cherished images of truth when those images prevent encounter with what exceeds them.\nContext: Eckhart's sermons on detachment, poverty of spirit, and knowing God beyond images.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260918@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260918T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260918T011200
SUMMARY:Opposites can coincide
DESCRIPTION:Nicholas of Cusa\nIdea: At the limits of ordinary comparison, oppositions generated by finite concepts may cease to behave as oppositions. Contradiction can sometimes mark the boundary of a conceptual instrument rather than the boundary of reality.\nContext: Cusa's 'coincidence of opposites' and learned ignorance.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260919@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260919T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260919T011200
SUMMARY:Beauty can demand labor
DESCRIPTION:Dante Gabriel Rossetti\nIdea: Beauty need not be an escape from difficult making. The finished image can conceal revision, discipline, failure, and return; enchantment and workmanship are not opposites.\nContext: Rossetti's intertwined practice of poetry, painting, revision, and reworking.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260920@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260920T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260920T011200
SUMMARY:Renunciation makes room
DESCRIPTION:Christina Rossetti\nIdea: Refusal is not always mere deprivation. Giving something up can create a form of interior space in which another allegiance, perception, or desire becomes possible.\nContext: A recurring structure in Rossetti's devotional poetry, where desire, restraint, loss, and transformed desire interact.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260921@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260921T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260921T011200
SUMMARY:Is the joke still a joke?
DESCRIPTION:Andy Kaufman\nIdea: A performance can destabilize the boundary between event and representation. When the audience cannot locate the frame, its own expectations become part of the material.\nContext: Kaufman's deliberate blurring of character, prank, performance, sincerity, and audience complicity.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260923@1am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260924T021000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260923T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260923T011200
SUMMARY:Invert mercy
DESCRIPTION:Isaac of Nineveh\n\nIDEA\nCompassion can refuse the arithmetic by which worthiness is calculated.\n\nINVERT\nReverse the usual priority for a moment. What becomes newly visible?\n\n\n\nCONTEXT\nAscetical Homilies. This entry is a compact conceptual paraphrase\, not a quotation.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260924@1am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260924T021000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260924T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260924T011200
SUMMARY:Invert absence
DESCRIPTION:John of the Cross\n\nIDEA\nThe loss of familiar consolation can expose whether desire seeks reality or merely a preferred experience of it.\n\nINVERT\nReverse the usual priority for a moment. What becomes newly visible?\n\nCONVERSATION\nAn accidental neighbor from the previous seven days: Isaac of Nineveh\, “Invert mercy.” That thought was working with mercy\; today’s works with desire. Put them beside each other and ask whether one changes the scale\, frame\, or stakes of the other. If the connection does not survive attention\, let it fail rather than manufacturing one.\n\nCONTEXT\nDark Night. This entry is a compact conceptual paraphrase\, not a quotation.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260925@1am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260924T021000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260925T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260925T011200
SUMMARY:Test desire
DESCRIPTION:Augustine of Hippo\n\nIDEA\nWhat we love gives direction and gravity to attention\, memory\, and action.\n\nTEST\nWhere does this idea fail\, and what does that failure reveal about its proper scope?\n\nCONVERSATION\nJohn of the Cross appeared within the previous seven days with “Invert absence.” Both thoughts touch desire\, but the overlap was accidental. Read them together without assuming agreement: does Augustine of Hippo deepen the earlier thought\, contradict its emphasis\, or reveal that the shared vocabulary conceals different projects?\n\nCONTEXT\nConfessions. This entry is a compact conceptual paraphrase\, not a quotation.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260926@1am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260924T021000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260926T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260926T011200
SUMMARY:Test mercy
DESCRIPTION:Isaac of Nineveh\n\nIDEA\nCompassion can refuse the arithmetic by which worthiness is calculated.\n\nTEST\nWhere does this idea fail\, and what does that failure reveal about its proper scope?\n\nCONVERSATION\nIsaac of Nineveh appeared within the previous seven days with “Invert mercy.” Both thoughts touch mercy\, but the overlap was accidental. Read them together without assuming agreement: does Isaac of Nineveh deepen the earlier thought\, contradict its emphasis\, or reveal that the shared vocabulary conceals different projects?\n\nCONTEXT\nAscetical Homilies. This entry is a compact conceptual paraphrase\, not a quotation.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260927@1am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260924T021000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260927T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260927T011200
SUMMARY:Test future
DESCRIPTION:Jürgen Moltmann\n\nIDEA\nThe future is not only what comes later. Anticipated possibility can interrupt the present and alter what counts as realistic.\n\nTEST\nWhere does this idea fail\, and what does that failure reveal about its proper scope?\n\nCONVERSATION\nAn accidental neighbor from the previous seven days: Isaac of Nineveh\, “Invert mercy.” That thought was working with mercy\; today’s works with hope. Put them beside each other and ask whether one changes the scale\, frame\, or stakes of the other. If the connection does not survive attention\, let it fail rather than manufacturing one.\n\nCONTEXT\nTheology of Hope. This entry is a compact conceptual paraphrase\, not a quotation.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260928@1am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260924T021000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260928T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260928T011200
SUMMARY:Invert mediation
DESCRIPTION:Maximus the Confessor\n\nIDEA\nA local relation can participate in reconciling larger divisions rather than remaining merely local.\n\nINVERT\nReverse the usual priority for a moment. What becomes newly visible?\n\nCONVERSATION\nAn accidental neighbor from the previous seven days: Augustine of Hippo\, “Test desire.” That thought was working with desire\; today’s works with relation. Put them beside each other and ask whether one changes the scale\, frame\, or stakes of the other. If the connection does not survive attention\, let it fail rather than manufacturing one.\n\nCONTEXT\nAmbigua. This entry is a compact conceptual paraphrase\, not a quotation.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260929@1am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260924T021000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260929T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260929T011200
SUMMARY:Invert repetition
DESCRIPTION:Søren Kierkegaard\n\nIDEA\nTrue repetition is not simple recurrence. What returns can become new because the person returning has changed.\n\nINVERT\nReverse the usual priority for a moment. What becomes newly visible?\n\nCONVERSATION\nAn accidental neighbor from the previous seven days: Isaac of Nineveh\, “Invert mercy.” That thought was working with mercy\; today’s works with time. Put them beside each other and ask whether one changes the scale\, frame\, or stakes of the other. If the connection does not survive attention\, let it fail rather than manufacturing one.\n\nCONTEXT\nRepetition. This entry is a compact conceptual paraphrase\, not a quotation.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260930@1am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260924T021000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260930T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260930T011200
SUMMARY:Contradiction can birth
DESCRIPTION:Jakob Böhme\n\nIDEA\nSome forms of generativity arise not after contradiction disappears but through the structured tension it creates.\n\nNOTICE\nWhat becomes visible if you treat this as a claim about perception rather than as a slogan?\n\nCONVERSATION\nAn accidental neighbor from the previous seven days: Søren Kierkegaard\, “Invert repetition.” That thought was working with time\; today’s works with paradox. Put them beside each other and ask whether one changes the scale\, frame\, or stakes of the other. If the connection does not survive attention\, let it fail rather than manufacturing one.\n\nCONTEXT\nTheosophical writings. This entry is a compact conceptual paraphrase\, not a quotation.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261001@1am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260924T021000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261001T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261001T011200
SUMMARY:Invert chance
DESCRIPTION:John Cage\n\nIDEA\nChance procedures can remove some habits of preference and allow structures the maker would not deliberately choose.\n\nINVERT\nReverse the usual priority for a moment. What becomes newly visible?\n\nCONVERSATION\nAn accidental neighbor from the previous seven days: Isaac of Nineveh\, “Test mercy.” That thought was working with mercy\; today’s works with making. Put them beside each other and ask whether one changes the scale\, frame\, or stakes of the other. If the connection does not survive attention\, let it fail rather than manufacturing one.\n\nCONTEXT\nchance operations. This entry is a compact conceptual paraphrase\, not a quotation.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261002@1am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260924T021000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261002T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261002T011200
SUMMARY:Test infinite
DESCRIPTION:Nicholas of Cusa\n\nIDEA\nFinite measures cannot simply be extrapolated until they become adequate to the infinite.\n\nTEST\nWhere does this idea fail\, and what does that failure reveal about its proper scope?\n\nCONVERSATION\nAn accidental neighbor from the previous seven days: John Cage\, “Invert chance.” That thought was working with making\; today’s works with scale. Put them beside each other and ask whether one changes the scale\, frame\, or stakes of the other. If the connection does not survive attention\, let it fail rather than manufacturing one.\n\nCONTEXT\nOn Learned Ignorance. This entry is a compact conceptual paraphrase\, not a quotation.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261003@1am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260924T021000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261003T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261003T011200
SUMMARY:Test ordinary
DESCRIPTION:George Herbert\n\nIDEA\nDomestic and commonplace things can become intellectually and spiritually articulate under sustained attention.\n\nTEST\nWhere does this idea fail\, and what does that failure reveal about its proper scope?\n\nCONVERSATION\nAn accidental neighbor from the previous seven days: Jakob Böhme\, “Contradiction can birth.” That thought was working with paradox\; today’s works with attention. Put them beside each other and ask whether one changes the scale\, frame\, or stakes of the other. If the connection does not survive attention\, let it fail rather than manufacturing one.\n\nCONTEXT\npoetry. This entry is a compact conceptual paraphrase\, not a quotation.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261004@1am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260924T021000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261004T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261004T011200
SUMMARY:What owns attention?
DESCRIPTION:Evagrius Ponticus\n\nIDEA\nA recurring thought can organize perception and action before argument ever begins.\n\nNOTICE\nWhat becomes visible if you treat this as a claim about perception rather than as a slogan?\n\nCONVERSATION\nGeorge Herbert appeared within the previous seven days with “Test ordinary.” Both thoughts touch attention\, but the overlap was accidental. Read them together without assuming agreement: does Evagrius Ponticus deepen the earlier thought\, contradict its emphasis\, or reveal that the shared vocabulary conceals different projects?\n\nCONTEXT\nPraktikos. This entry is a compact conceptual paraphrase\, not a quotation.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261005@1am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260924T021000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261005T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261005T011200
SUMMARY:Test habit
DESCRIPTION:Anthony the Great\n\nIDEA\nA new place cannot by itself remove the patterns one carries into it.\n\nTEST\nWhere does this idea fail\, and what does that failure reveal about its proper scope?\n\nCONVERSATION\nAn accidental neighbor from the previous seven days: Jakob Böhme\, “Contradiction can birth.” That thought was working with paradox\; today’s works with practice. Put them beside each other and ask whether one changes the scale\, frame\, or stakes of the other. If the connection does not survive attention\, let it fail rather than manufacturing one.\n\nCONTEXT\ndesert tradition. This entry is a compact conceptual paraphrase\, not a quotation.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261006@1am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260924T021000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261006T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261006T011200
SUMMARY:Test depth
DESCRIPTION:Origen\n\nIDEA\nA text may yield different levels of meaning as the reader and the question change.\n\nTEST\nWhere does this idea fail\, and what does that failure reveal about its proper scope?\n\nCONVERSATION\nAn accidental neighbor from the previous seven days: Søren Kierkegaard\, “Invert repetition.” That thought was working with time\; today’s works with reading. Put them beside each other and ask whether one changes the scale\, frame\, or stakes of the other. If the connection does not survive attention\, let it fail rather than manufacturing one.\n\nCONTEXT\nOn First Principles / homilies. This entry is a compact conceptual paraphrase\, not a quotation.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261007@1am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260924T021000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261007T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261007T011200
SUMMARY:Test center
DESCRIPTION:Simone Weil\n\nIDEA\nThe self's demand to occupy the center of reality can be loosened so that what is not-self becomes more perceptible.\n\nTEST\nWhere does this idea fail\, and what does that failure reveal about its proper scope?\n\nCONVERSATION\nAn accidental neighbor from the previous seven days: Evagrius Ponticus\, “What owns attention?.” That thought was working with attention\; today’s works with self. Put them beside each other and ask whether one changes the scale\, frame\, or stakes of the other. If the connection does not survive attention\, let it fail rather than manufacturing one.\n\nCONTEXT\nGravity and Grace. This entry is a compact conceptual paraphrase\, not a quotation.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261008@1am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260924T021000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261008T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261008T011200
SUMMARY:Test cell
DESCRIPTION:Syncletica of Alexandria\n\nIDEA\nConfinement can make inner agitation more visible because fewer external changes are available to disguise it.\n\nTEST\nWhere does this idea fail\, and what does that failure reveal about its proper scope?\n\nCONVERSATION\nSimone Weil appeared within the previous seven days with “Test center.” Both thoughts touch self\, but the overlap was accidental. Read them together without assuming agreement: does Syncletica of Alexandria deepen the earlier thought\, contradict its emphasis\, or reveal that the shared vocabulary conceals different projects?\n\nCONTEXT\ndesert tradition. This entry is a compact conceptual paraphrase\, not a quotation.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261009@1am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260924T021000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261009T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261009T011200
SUMMARY:Tools can reverse ends
DESCRIPTION:Ivan Illich\n\nIDEA\nTools and institutions can pass a threshold where producing more of a service begins to frustrate the human good they were built to serve.\n\nNOTICE\nWhat becomes visible if you treat this as a claim about perception rather than as a slogan?\n\nCONVERSATION\nAn accidental neighbor from the previous seven days: George Herbert\, “Test ordinary.” That thought was working with attention\; today’s works with tools. Put them beside each other and ask whether one changes the scale\, frame\, or stakes of the other. If the connection does not survive attention\, let it fail rather than manufacturing one.\n\nCONTEXT\nTools for Conviviality / Medical Nemesis. This entry is a compact conceptual paraphrase\, not a quotation.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261010@1am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260924T021000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261010T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261010T011200
SUMMARY:Form can think
