SUMMARY:Become singular
DESCRIPTION:Søren Kierkegaard\n\nIDEA\nA crowd can lend borrowed conviction. Becoming a self requires personally inhabiting what one takes to be true.\n\nNOTICE\nWhat becomes visible if you treat this as a claim about perception rather than as a slogan?\n\nCONVERSATION\nFlannery O’Connor appeared within the previous seven days with “Invert goodness.” Both thoughts touch self\, but the overlap was accidental. Read them together without assuming agreement: does Søren Kierkegaard deepen the earlier thought\, contradict its emphasis\, or reveal that the shared vocabulary conceals different projects?\n\nCONTEXT\nwritings on the single individual. This entry is a compact conceptual paraphrase\, not a quotation.
