SUMMARY:Learn your ignorance
DESCRIPTION:Nicholas of Cusa\n\nIDEA\nRecognizing the structure of one's ignorance can be a positive form of knowledge rather than mere deficiency.\n\nNOTICE\nWhat becomes visible if you treat this as a claim about perception rather than as a slogan?\n\nCONVERSATION\nNicholas of Cusa appeared within the previous seven days with “Test ignorance.” Both thoughts touch unknowing\, but the overlap was accidental. Read them together without assuming agreement: does Nicholas of Cusa deepen the earlier thought\, contradict its emphasis\, or reveal that the shared vocabulary conceals different projects?\n\nCONTEXT\nOn Learned Ignorance. This entry is a compact conceptual paraphrase\, not a quotation.
