SUMMARY:Invert excess
DESCRIPTION:Pseudo-Dionysius\n\nIDEA\nWhat exceeds the capacity of cognition can appear as darkness\, not because nothing is there but because too much exceeds the instrument.\n\nINVERT\nReverse the usual priority for a moment. What becomes newly visible?\n\nCONVERSATION\nAn accidental neighbor from the previous seven days: Nicholas of Cusa\, “Test opposites.” That thought was working with paradox\; today’s works with unknowing. Put them beside each other and ask whether one changes the scale\, frame\, or stakes of the other. If the connection does not survive attention\, let it fail rather than manufacturing one.\n\nCONTEXT\nMystical Theology. This entry is a compact conceptual paraphrase\, not a quotation.
