SUMMARY:Invert wonder
DESCRIPTION:G. K. Chesterton\n\nIDEA\nThe ordinary can become astonishing when familiar existence is seen as something that need not have been.\n\nINVERT\nReverse the usual priority for a moment. What becomes newly visible?\n\nCONVERSATION\nAn accidental neighbor from the previous seven days: Pseudo-Dionysius\, “Invert excess.” That thought was working with unknowing\; today’s works with wonder. Put them beside each other and ask whether one changes the scale\, frame\, or stakes of the other. If the connection does not survive attention\, let it fail rather than manufacturing one.\n\nCONTEXT\nOrthodoxy. This entry is a compact conceptual paraphrase\, not a quotation.
