SUMMARY:Silence is not empty
DESCRIPTION:John Cage\n\nIDEA\nSilence becomes crowded when attention stops deciding in advance which sounds count.\n\nNOTICE\nWhat becomes visible if you treat this as a claim about perception rather than as a slogan?\n\nCONVERSATION\nEvagrius Ponticus appeared within the previous seven days with “Name the thought-pattern.” Both thoughts touch attention\, but the overlap was accidental. Read them together without assuming agreement: does John Cage deepen the earlier thought\, contradict its emphasis\, or reveal that the shared vocabulary conceals different projects?\n\nCONTEXT\n4′33″ and writings on silence. This entry is a compact conceptual paraphrase\, not a quotation.
