SUMMARY:Invert night
DESCRIPTION:John of the Cross\n\nIDEA\nPeriods of obscurity can dismantle inadequate forms of knowing without yet supplying replacements.\n\nINVERT\nReverse the usual priority for a moment. What becomes newly visible?\n\nCONVERSATION\nPseudo-Dionysius appeared within the previous seven days with “Invert excess.” Both thoughts touch unknowing\, but the overlap was accidental. Read them together without assuming agreement: does John of the Cross deepen the earlier thought\, contradict its emphasis\, or reveal that the shared vocabulary conceals different projects?\n\nCONTEXT\nDark Night. This entry is a compact conceptual paraphrase\, not a quotation.
