SUMMARY:Invert suffering
DESCRIPTION:Jürgen Moltmann\n\nIDEA\nAny account of reality that makes present suffering feel final or necessary has surrendered something essential to hope.\n\nINVERT\nReverse the usual priority for a moment. What becomes newly visible?\n\nCONVERSATION\nAn accidental neighbor from the previous seven days: William Blake\, “Invert energy.” That thought was working with desire\; today’s works with hope. Put them beside each other and ask whether one changes the scale\, frame\, or stakes of the other. If the connection does not survive attention\, let it fail rather than manufacturing one.\n\nCONTEXT\nThe Crucified God. This entry is a compact conceptual paraphrase\, not a quotation.
